#heading(level: 1)[Preface]
#v(0.5em)
‘Study to show thyself approved unto God, a workman that needeth not be ashamed, rightly dividing the word of truth.’ II Timothy, Ch.2, v.15. This book is intended for all those who are keenly interested in the roots of the Indo- European system of languages: for teachers, students, and lovers of Sanskrit, Greek, Latin and modern languages, including English. Designed to be complete in itself, this book includes the ancient Indian text of the Dhatu-Patha, literally ‘a recital of roots’, complete with its own dictionary, essential grammatical aphorisms and rules, helpful hints and many examples from the ancient Vedic and classical texts. Anyone who wishes to enquire into the deeper significance of words which form the great traditions of India and Europe will find the Dhatu-Patha an invaluable and rewarding guide. The Dhatu-Patha was originally intended as an oral recital of roots and their meanings; until quite recently in India, it was customary for educated persons to be able to recite the entire text from memory; this is important, as ultimately meaning is contained in sound, Users of the book are therefore encouraged to penetrate meaning by sounding the roots with their meanings, either aloud or in the mind, for only in this manner is correct use made of the Dhatu-Patha. S.R.H. & P.G.H. vii #h(1fr) #text(7pt, fill: luma(120))[[S. 6]]

