#heading(level: 1)[Introduction]
#v(0.5em)
‘In the beginning was the Word...’ The entire Creation is brought into being by ‘the Word’ and this concept is common to all great cultures. This mighty idea is instantly recognizable to the individual, but it is difficult to appreciate its profundity and even harder to comprehend it. Yet all would acknowledge the supreme importance attaching to verbal communication and to the penetration and comprehension of meaning in all aspects of Creation. Genesis (Ch.11, v.1) states: ‘And the whole earth was of one language and of one speech’, but goes on to describe the confusion of tongues in the parable of the Tower of Babel, where ‘the Lord did there confound the language of all the earth’ (v.9). The idea of one language strikes a responsive chord in many, since humanity is a single family and the human experience is common to all men; the feeling of existence, experienced by all as the necessary condition of their very being, is universal. All languages come into being to express this universal feeling of existence, to differentiate the manifold aspects of Creation, and to communicate Truth, Knowledge and Experience. Hence the study of language in all its aspects has from the beginning been a central study for mankind, For as St. Basil said: ‘The beginning of teaching is speech, and syllables and words are parts of speech’ (de Anima, Chapter 1.2); and as Bhartrhari stated: ‘In this world no comprehension is possible except as accompanied by speech; all knowledge shines by means of speech... It is speech that binds all knowledge of arts and crafts; everything, when it is produced, is differentiated through it’ (Vakyapadiya, Ch.1, vv.123 and 125). In his introduction to the Oxford Sanskrit-English dictionary (p.xii), Sir Monier Monier- Williams draws attention ‘to the fact that the Hindus are perhaps the only nation, except the Greeks, who have investigated independently and in a truly scientific manner the general laws which govern the evolution of language.’ He continues by describing how ‘every single word in the highest type of language (called samskrta) is first evolved out of a primary dhdtu ~ प Sanskrit term usually translated as ‘root’, but applicable to any primordial constituent substance, whether of words, or rocks, or living organisms — and then, being so evolved, goes through a process of ‘putting together’ by the combination of other elementary constituents.” This broad definition of dhdtu is very similar to the Greek concept of stoicheion (element or root), which comprises both simple sounds of the voice as the first elements of language, and also the component parts of matter, and even the elements of knowledge and the sciences. In view of this wider connotation of the function of dhdtu at the level of Creation, it is surprising that such an important text as the Dhdtu-Patha has not been more widely studied in the West, being the first foundation of the Indo-European root system. The Dhatu-Patha (‘A Recital of Roots’) The Dhatu-Patha is a unique tool for penetrating the subtler meanings of language and for regulating its correct use. Moreover, it is at once a lantern for illuminating the texts of the #h(1fr) #text(7pt, fill: luma(120))[[S. 10]]

#v(0.4em)
Vedic tradition known as Sandtana Dharma, the ‘Eternal Way’, and a touchstone of their validity. It should be understood at the outset that the word ‘language’ is most certainly not restricted to Sanskrit, but embraces the entire Indo-European family of languages, including Greek, Latin, Anglo-Saxon, Old High German, the Slavonic languages and all the modern Euro- pean derivations. Sir Monier Monier-Williams states that Sanskrit is ‘the best guide to the structure of Greek as well as of every other member of the Aryan or Indo-European family - 9 language in short which is the very key-stone of the science of comparative philology’ (ibid. p-xi), and he calls it ‘the eldest sister of these languages’ (ibid. p.xii). There are indeed good reasons for its being designated ‘the best guide’. In order that the Veda, the repository and ultimate authority of the wisdom of India, might be studied and preserved, the ancient scholars evolved complete and detailed systems, each a science in its own right: firstly, phonetics (Siksha); then rhythm and metre (Chandas); etymology and meaning (Nirukta); grammar (Vydkarana); astronomy (Jyotisha); and ritual practices (Kalpa). These are the six Veddriga or ‘limbs of the Veda’. The scholars revealed the science of phonetics by identifying a natural gamut of human sounds. These sounds are not to be understood as Sanskrit sounds or the sounds of any other particular language, but are sounds of ‘the natural alphabet’, by which term it is inferred that there is a complete : available to all men, in all places and at all times. Thus Sokrates observes that ‘the Sound which issues through the mouth, whether of an individual or of all men, is one and yet infinite” (Philebus 17. b3-4), and then describes the process of distinguishing the sounds of ‘the natural alphabet’ (ibid. 18; cf. Kratylos 424f). Hence Sanskrit, furnished with the most extensive alphabet, has greater potential for precise and detailed expression. Indeed, the laws of grammar as embodied in Pdnini’s Ashtadhydyi (literally ‘Eight Meditations’) are also acknowledged to be the most complete and refined exposition of the science of grammar, and could only be expressed in the fully-formed Sanskrit alphabet. Now the science of grammar (vydkarana) is entirely dhdtu-based, and the Dhatu-Patha is a recital of those dhdtu identified and dealt with in that science, and is the progenitrix of the root systems of the Indo-European languages. The corollary of this important fact is that the Dhatu-Patha is valid as an authority for helping to ascertain meaning in any of those languages, although allowance must often be made for the fact that words may change their usage and their meanings over the centuries. However, consider for example the word ‘instability’, which readily breaks down into three components: the negative prefix ‘in-’; the suffix \*-ity’; and the word ‘stable’, which means ‘steady, reliable, constant’. The new word thus formed now indicates a state of unsteadiness or inconstancy. But within the basic word ‘stable’, there is another element, the dhdtu shthd, which indicates the ‘cessation of movement’. This simple element is the basis of many other words such as ‘stand’, ‘standard’, ‘statue’, ‘station’, all of which share in the common basic concept of lack of movement. The dhatu is therefore the basic element of a large family of words having a common essential 2 #h(1fr) #text(7pt, fill: luma(120))[[S. 11]]

#v(0.4em)
meaning. Most Sanskrit dictionaries are laid out according to these families of dhatu, and the deepest penetration and understanding of language will be greatly facilitated by an acquaintance with and knowledge of these most subtle and basic elements. What is a dhatu? Sir Monier Monier-Williams’ Sanskrit-English Dictionary defines dhatu as: ‘layer, stratum, constituent part, ingredient, element, primitive matter, a constituent element or essential ingredient of the body, primary element of the earth, i.e. metal, mineral, ore, element of words, i.e. grammatical or verbal root or stem’ (M-W, p.513, col.3. ~ 513/3). (Note: inclusion of ‘stem’ is debatable.) V.S. Apte’s Practical Sanskrit-English Dictionary defines dhdtu in the same terms (A 524/3), but adds ‘the soul’ and ‘The Supreme Spirit’ as meanings (cf. Vishnu Saharsranama Bhashya, Sloka 18). It can be readily appreciated that dhdtu has a wide range of meaning beyond the purely grammatical, and not surprisingly this subject has given rise to extensive controversy amongst grammarians, logicians, and philosophers. For example, the debates range from whether grammatical dhdtu assume these wider meanings or are merely, at worst, theoretical abstractions. However, the preferred route of the authors through this endless controversy is to consult the Dhatu-Patha itself. The form and use of the Dhatu-Patha are described in the next two sections; for the moment it is enough to know that the Dhdtu-Patha gives a dhatvartha (‘indication of meaning’) for each dhatu; the word dhdatvartha is itself a compound of dhdtu and artha, where artha means ‘indication of use’ (M-W 90/2). Now the dhatvartha for the dhatu of the word dhdatu itself, namely dhd (D-P 26/2 —shown as dudhdn, but see (iv) on page 6). is dhdrana + poshanayoh, a dual compound indicating the activity of ‘holding, bearing, keeping (in remembrance)’ and ‘the act of nourishing, fostering, supporting’ (Dhdtvartha Dictionary, \"0 D’, Nos. 283, 354). According to tradition, these definitions give the essential activities in which dhdtu engage. and in this instance they are clearly essential and appropriate, just as the acorn is to the oak, to the whole range of meanings of the word dhdtu. A technical definition of the primary dhdtu, approximately 2,200 of which are listed in the Dhatu-Patha, might be: the uninflected, irreducible, minimal element of a word, generally monosyllabic (though two and, more rarely, three syllables are sometimes present), and always verbal in nature, i.e. expressive of activity. #h(1fr) #text(7pt, fill: luma(120))[[S. 12]]

#v(0.4em)
Finally, the relationship between dhatu and sphotha needs to be considered. The dictionary definition of sphotha (M-W 1270/3) is: ‘bursting, opening, expansion, disclosure; ... roar; (in philosophy) sound (conceived as eternal, indivisible and creative); the external and imperceptible element of sounds and words and the real vehicle of the idea which bursts or flashes on the mind when a sound is uttered \[ascribed to Patanjali\].’ The dhdtu for sphotha is sphut (D-P 6/1, 35/1 and 45/1), which has two dhdtvartha, namely vikasana and bhedana (D D Nos. 504 and 434), meaning respectively ‘developing, expanding, opening, blowing’ and ‘breaking, splitting, piercing, dividing, separating, causing to flow’. When the desire arises for the expression of a particular aspect of the totality of Being, the function of the sphotha is to bring about the expansion of this potentiality into manifestation, 1.6. speech, in which process there is of necessity a differentiation of consciousness, into such components as subject, object and instrument, i.e. into the various parts of speech and sentence. Within this process, the sphosha acts as catalyst by activating the pre-existing, but as yet unmanifest, dhdtu. In this expansion, the dhdtu receives certain additions in the form of pratyaya, for which the dictionary definition (M-W 673/3) is: ‘belief, firm conviction, trust, faith, assurance or certainty ... a subsequent sound or letter, an affix or suffix to roots (forming verbs, substantives, adjectives and all derivatives) etc.” In essence, the pratyaya embodies the consciousness in a fully-finished form, of which the grammatical equivalent is the completed word operating in the sentence (cf. Vishnu Sahasrandma Bhashya, sloka 23). The form of the Dhatu-Pdatha The text of the Dhdtu-Patha is divided into two sections: spreads ॥ to 52 are the recital of dhdatu and their corresponding dhatvartha, and spreads 53 to 99 form the Index of dhdtu as found in that first section. Each spread (in both sections) is divided into four main columns; in the first two columns the dhdtu are on the left and the dhdtvartha are on the right in each column, and the transliteration appears in the final two columns. The Index follows the same basic pattern but contains additional information on each root: The first minor column in the Index is an alphabetical listing of dhdtu. The second gives the name of one of the ten possible gana or classes of declension to pg the dhdtu belongs. the name of each class being formed from the first dhdtu in that class. #h(1fr) #text(7pt, fill: luma(120))[[S. 13]]

#v(0.4em)
The third gives the voice: parasmai (active voice or transitive), atmane (middle or passive voice or intransitive), or ubhayatah (either active or middle/passive voice); ‘this distinction, however, seems to be very little observed in practice’ (M.R. Kale, A Higher Sanskrit Grammar, v. Select Bibliography, 399). The fourth shows whether the dhdtu accepts augment -i in its expansion: if it does, it is set, literally ‘with if’, and the indication is se; e.g. dhdtu pas becomes pasyati, ‘he sees’, with the -i- forming the -ya- sound when eliding with -ati, the pratyaya ending. If it does not, it is anit, literally ‘without if, and the indication is a; e.g. dhdtu bhi and as become bhavati and asti respectively, both meaning ‘he is’. The last two minor columns give the page and column number in the Dhdtu-Patha where each dhatu may be found with its dhatvartha, Although the technical definition of dhdtu is ‘generally monosyllabic’, dhdtu are mostly shown with anubandha suffixes, literally suffixes ‘tied behind’, and, where applicable, with prefixes. The anubandha suffixes, and the prefixes, form a system of coding adopted by grammarians to indicate a specific direction in the expansion of the dhdtu for determining the pratyaya. A full list of the anubandha suffixes and their function is given in A Dictionary of Sanskrit Grammar (v. Select Bibliography, 69), under reference if, and in most Sanskrit grammars. The prefixes, which should be removed in order to determine the monosyllabic dhatu, comprise ni, tu, du and nasalised vowels (v. Panini, 1.3.5 and 1.3.2). It should be mentioned that the effect of upasarga, or ‘prefixes’, on the meaning of verbs is crucial: Appendix | expands on this subject. The ten gana or classes represent the ten possible declensions of Sanskrit verbs, and each class has sub-groups based on distinctions in the expansion of the declension. The text itself gives the name of the first dhdtu, commencing with the introductory statement, atha, and the name of the last dhatu, the number of dhatu and the voice of the dhatu for each group. At the end of the recital of each group there is a statement indicating termination of that section, namely iti, together with the accentuation applicable to the dhdatu and to their anubandha endings. In this way the correct mode of sounding each syllable of the entire dhdtu is preserved. In the realm of grammar, the particular class into which a dhdtu is placed is determined by the formation of the stem of the present tense, which is the most frequently used stem for the other tenses throughout each declension. However, on reciting the first dhdtu, bhi, the reader is aware that the text commences with the most fundamental activity of all Creation ~ Being. Therefore, it is useful to be aware of the possibility that the ordering of dhdtu may intimate a deeper significance underlying the purely grammatical considerations: Appendix 11 proposes the hypothesis that the classification of dhdtu may have sequential significance. This hypothesis may help to explain the phenomenon of dhatu which appear in more than one class or more than once in the same class, either with the same or with different dhdtvartha. Appendix II, however, should be viewed as exploratory in nature. 5 #h(1fr) #text(7pt, fill: luma(120))[[S. 14]]

#v(0.4em)
How to use the Dhatu-Patha The Transliteration Key preceding this Introduction is the starting point for the user, for \\t contains not only the transliteration symbols as used in this edition, but also the order of the Sanskrit alphabet, which is the basis of the order of the Dhdtu-Patha Index on spreads 53 ty 99, of the Dhatvartha Dictionary, which follows that Index, and of the order of Sir Moniér Monier-Williams’ Sanskrit-English Dictionary, in which entries are both listed alphabetically and under their respective dhdtu; this dictionary thus provides a vital link between Sanskrit words and their dhdtu, For those working with this Dhdtu-Pdtha text and Sanskrit-English Dictionary, the following grammatical rules, which are given with the relevant reference ty Panini’s Ashtdadhyayi in brackets, must be taken into account in order to identify the variations between text and dictionary: (i) sh changes to s; e.g. for shthd in the Dhdtu-Patha read sthd in the dictionary (Panini, 6.1.64). (ii) machanges to na; e.g. for nad in the Dhatu-Patha read nad in the dictionary (Panini, 6.1.65). (iii) For-iasanubandha ending in the Dhdtu-Patha, insert na after the vowel of the dhdtk; e.g. for madi in the Dhatu-Patha, read mand in the dictionary (Pdnini, 7.1.58). (iv) Remember that dhdtu are generally monosyllabic, and so remove prefixes and suffixes; e.g. for ramu in the Dhatu-Patha, read ram in the dictionary; for dudhdf in the Dhdtu-Patha, read dhd in the dictionary. (v) Final mute nasals should be dropped; e.g. for dhdtu in and in, read i (2171401९) and i (parasmai) respectively in the dictionary; for dhdtu dukrf in the Dhatu-Patha, read kr (ubhayatah) in the dictionary. Note: du drops out here under rule (iv) above. Each dhatu or group of dhdtu is shown in the main body of the text on spreads 1 to 52 in the form of a dhdtu-siitra e.g.: spread 1, column 1; bha (dhatu) sattayam (dhatvartha) In this instance the dhdtvartha is a single word, expressed in the seventh or locative case, which is the case used for most of the dhdtvartha. However, dhatvartha may take the form ofa dual or multiple compound, and to assist the user in such instances the transliteration breaks the compounds down into individual words; these are indicated by hyphens, and for further assistance where sandhi or crasis (meaning euphonic combination of two letters) takes place, the original form has been indicated by means of brackets; e.g. on spread 1 for dhdatu nos. 6 and 7, the dhdtvartha starts yacnopatapa- etc., which is shown as ydcno(a) -(u)patapa, indicating thereby that the original words were ydcna and upatapa, which is their form in the 6 #h(1fr) #text(7pt, fill: luma(120))[[S. 15]]

#v(0.4em)
Dhatvartha Dictionary. The dhatvartha is generally expressed in the locative case to indicate that use of the dhdtu will be found in the activity(ies) or condition(s) described by the dhdtvartha. Consequently, an expanded form of the dhdtu-sditra example above is: You will find dhatu bhi in the activities of ‘existence, being, goodness, excellence’ and ‘reality’. The dhatvartha in the Dhdatvartha Dictionary is expressed in the pratipadika (‘uninflected’) form of the word; hence, in the above example, the dhatvartha sattayam appears as satta (D D No. 611). This change of form is generally easy to recognise with a little practice, but for further clarification the following locative case endings should be noted: (i) Singular endings occur in five forms: For -e ending, read -a. For -dydm ending, read -d. For -ydm ending, read -i. For -au ending, read -i. For -i ending, remove -i. (ii) All dual compounds end in -yoh, which is removed. (iii) All plural endings, irrespective of gender, have -shu or -eshu, which are removed, e.g. for a compound ending in -gatishu, read -gati; for a compound ending in -darsaneshu, read -darsana, etc. The Dhdatvartha Dictionary gives the dhdtvartha individually, in transliteration and in alphabetical order; then, for each word, it supplies a range of meanings selected from Sir Monier Monier-Williams’ (M-W) dictionary, augmented where appropriate with additional meanings selected from V.S. Apte’s (A) dictionary. Beneath these meanings, the dhdtu of the dhatvartha is given, together with its voice and class and transliteration of the dhdtvartha of this dhdtu, to enable the user to penetrate further into the meaning of the original dhdtu by looking up this second dhatvartha in the Dhatvartha Dictionary. In most cases, the entry is completed by appropriate quotations from the ancient literature of India and Europe, displaying the dhdatvartha in significant use in the Indo-European languages. Finally, the dhdtvartha are significant words in their own right within the Vedic tradition and can be considered to possess the extraordinary power necessary for holding the meaning of the more important words and concepts. To illustrate this point, Appendix III contains examples of the function performed by dhdatvartha; these are selected from the authoritative 7 #h(1fr) #text(7pt, fill: luma(120))[[S. 16]]

#v(0.4em)
commentaries made on the Vedic texts by Sarikara, who frequently quotes from the Dhatu- ; itself, taking it as an authority which required no further qualification. How to use the Excursus The Excursus follows the order of the Greek alphabet. Consequently, although many Latin, Anglo-Saxon, and English words have the same initial letter as the equivalent Greek word, it is sometimes necessary to scan the pages for these languages; also certain words in these later languages which have no equivalent Greek form may be found at the end of the Excursus. Where possible, a verb has been selected in preference to a noun, for, as has been stated above, the dhdtu are essentially verbal in meaning, i.e. they are linked to activity. Moreover, the Excursus would have become inordinately long if all the words from a particular root had been shown, and so the user must refer to his own knowledge of these languages, or, if necessary, refer to a dictionary; e.g. for dhatu shthd is shown histemi, sto, standan and stand. The number of words in each language from this root alone would require several pages, and so the user is reminded to think in terms of the essential verb (or noun) to which the word under investigation is related. Proper names and nouns are sometimes included, particularly where the connection might not be immediately apparent. A transliteration is given for each dhdtu, but no Sanskrit words are given, as Sir Monier Monier-Williams’ Dictionary, being dhatu-based, can readily supply these, and the number of words for certain dhdtu would produce excessively long entries. No attempt has been made to deal with onomatopoeic words or indeclinables, particularly as numerals (from 2 to 99), prepositions, and pronouns in all languages under consideration are very similar and clearly related. However, it must be stated that most indeclinables do not arise from the dhdtu listed in the Dhdtu-Patha, although some can be traced back to dhdtu; e.g. Tuesday in English is Tiwes daeg in Anglo-Saxon, and Tiwes is from Latin deus, from Greek Zetis, from Sanskrit dyaus, from dhdtu divu (D-P 28/1). Whilst an enquiry into the laws governing phonetic changes is well beyond the scope of this work, it is remarkable how often phonetic changes take place within the same varga, or family, i.e. gutturals, palatals etc., as set out in the ‘Transliteration Key’; e.g. ‘physics’ in English is from Greek phusis, from dhdtu bhi, where the aspirated bha sound has moved to the aspirated pha sound within the labial family. In identifying the relevant dhdtu for the words included in the Excursus, Sir Monier Monier- Williams’ dictionary has proven reliable and useful, as has the work of Georg Curtius (v. Select Bibliography). In fact, it is most surprising that nineteenth-century scholars, who performed such outstanding work in compiling dictionaries and formulating etymological principles, should not have made greater use of the Dhdatu-Patha. Curtius gives an interesting insight into the aspirations of his contemporaries: “The most neglected side of Etymology is still that of the doctrine of meaning, the very side which lies nearest to special classical students. To trace the use of a word, or a family of words, historically through one language, 8 #h(1fr) #text(7pt, fill: luma(120))[[S. 17]]

#v(0.4em)
or again, to arrive at points of view for a wider consideration of the changes of meaning, these are tasks to which, apparently, the present generation feels little drawn’ (18). Only derivations that appear beyond reasonable doubt and are accepted by at least one authority (either Curtius, Monier-Williams, Liddell & Scott, Lewis & Short, Skeat or Onions) have been included in the Excursus. There is one important exception, namely lokr, which is dealt with more fully in Appendix V (v. verse 1). Illustrative examples from the Dhdatu-Patha Two important dhdtu, which have philosophical connotations, will now be examined in greater detail. The dictionary definition for the word jrdna is: ‘Knowing, becoming acquainted with. Knowledge, especially the Higher Knowledge derived from meditation on the one Universal Spirit’ (M-W, 426/1). Dhatu jn is recited three times in the Dhatu-Patha: dhatvartha voice gana marana toshana ni\$Gmaneshu = (D-P. 17/1) parasmai bva® avabodhane (D-P 40/1) parasmai krya niyoge (D-P 45/1) ubhayatah cura” A glance at these activities through which jndna will be discovered is most instructive from the viewpoint of the adhydtmavidyd (‘Science of Self-Knowledge’). True knowledge, says the Veda, is Knowledge of the Self: For this knowledge to arise, all that relates to non-Self, ignorance, separation and all forms of limitation must be given up, rooted out and destroyed ~ mdrana (D D No. 449). This process proceeds step by step, each step leading to greater fulfilment and satisfaction — toshana (D D No. 255). The steps on the way are measured by the repetition of recourse to the experience of the bliss of the Self, known deep within — niSamaneshu (D D No. 305). The Teaching is also necessary: the student “sits at the feet of the master”, and the discourse gives rise to knowledge — avabodhana (D D No. 53). Such knowledge arises in niyoga (D D No. 299), an inner union arising from the natural application of the knowledge. #h(1fr) #text(7pt, fill: luma(120))[[S. 18]]

#v(0.4em)
Examining a related concept, consider the numerous words arising from dhdtu vid. Words such as veda (‘knowledge’) and vidyd (‘science’) in Sanskrit, oida (‘I know’) and eiddy (‘form’) in Greek, video (‘I know’) in Latin, witan in Anglo-Saxon and wit in English, all arise from this dhatu. Dhatu vid has the following entries in the Dhdatu-Patha:— dhatvartha voice gana 14/1९ (D-P 25/1) parasmai ada’ sattayam (D-P 29/2) 21771471 dv vicdrane (D-P 37/2) dtmane ru’ cetana-Gkhydna-nivaseshu (D-P 44/2) 0771471८ cura’ labhe (D-P 36/2) ubhayatah tuda’ N.B.: the final dhdtu (D-P 36/2) in the Index is given as vidlr, where /r is the anubandha ending that is dropped, giving dhdtu vid; however, it is number three in sequential order. The Dhatu-Patha informs us that use of dhdtu vid is found in knowledge — jfdne. This knowledge is found in one’s own Being ~ sattdydm, for truly ‘to Be’ is ‘to Know’: satyam jfAdnamanantam Brahma, meaning ‘The Self is Truth, Knowledge, Infinite’ (Taittiriya Upanishad, 2.1). Thus, veda is attained and a man discovers the knowledge ~ (40/14 (D D No. 486). But this true knowledge must be differentiated from all that is false and ignorant within us, by the process of discrimination — vicdrana (D D No. 506). The process of discrimination by which one comes to the true knowledge of all the forms of Creation is given in the compound dhdtvartha: — cetana ~ dkhydna — nivdsa: Nivasa (D D No. 302) means ‘to inhabit’, ‘to dwell within’, with ni- having the sense of ‘deep within’. A man must not stop at the outer form, for the knowledge of the Universal Self is the innermost essence; this inward direction enables a man to cognize the forms through and through, thus knowing the essence in unity as well as in the harmony of all its parts. The accurate enumeration of all aspects of a form in all its relationships is dkhydna(D D No. 70). And the whole process is dependent on consciousness; it is pervaded by consciousness; 10 #h(1fr) #text(7pt, fill: luma(120))[[S. 19]]

#v(0.4em)
and it is the conscious being which is revealed ~ cetana (D D No. 227). In order to demonstrate how the dhatu of language may help as guides to meaning in the Indo- European family of languages in general, similar examples may be taken from two Homeric words. It has long been realized that the first words of the Iliad and the Odyssey held a special significance (v. Chapman's Odyssey, Epistle Dedicatorie). The liad’s first word is menin (the accusative of menis), meaning ‘anger’, which comes from dhdtu mari (D-P 26/1), which has dhatvartha mana, meaning: ‘opinion, notion, conception, idea; purpose, wish, design; self- conceit, arrogance, pride; consideration, regard, respect, honour; a wounded sense of honour, anger or indignation excited by jealousy (esp. in women), caprice, sulking’ (M-W, 809/1), which is a good description of the wrath of Achilles that was to lead to the death of Patroklos and Hektor, and finally his own destruction. The Odyssey’s first word is andra (the accusative of andros), meaning ‘man’ (i.e. Odysseus), which comes from dhdatu nr (v. Excursus); this has for its dhatvartha the single word naya (D-P 17/1), and the dictionary definitions (D D No. 286) are very appropriate for the homeward journey of Odysseus, ‘the man of many devices’: ‘leading (of an army), conduct, behaviour, good management, polity, wisdom, reason; principle, system, method, doctrine; a kind of game and a sort of dice or men for playing it’ (M-W, 528/2). A word of caution It is not always immediately apparent how the dhdtvartha meaning and the word under investigation are connected, and often a moment’s reflection or further investigation is required. For example, the English verb ‘to move’ (and Latin moveo, Greek ameibo) derives from dhatu miva; the dhatvartha for miva is sthaulya (D D No. 654), meaning ‘stoutness, bigness, largeness, thickness, grossness, denseness. Excessive size or length. Doltishness, density of intellect’. It is difficult to see any immediately apparent connection with movement. In the dictionaries, sthaurin and sthirin, meaning ‘pack-horse’, given under the same dhdatu shtha as sthaulya, were closely related, for a pack-horse is both large and a powerful symbol of movement, but there was no available authority to link the two. However, an answer may be provided by the Upanishads, which call the gross physical world sthila (M-W, 1266/1) — the property of which is ever to be in movement, whether as a substance, or as an organism, or even as the Great Orb itself. Accentuation There are three svara (accents) in Sanskrit. They are termed udatta, anudatta, and svarita, and it is possible that they correspond to the Greek acute, grave, and circumflex accents. The relevant sutrani in Panini’s Ashthddyayi are: (1) ucchairudattah (1.2.21), ‘sounding high’. 11 #h(1fr) #text(7pt, fill: luma(120))[[S. 20]]

#v(0.4em)
(1) nicchairanudatah (1.2.30), ‘sounding low’. . (ili) samaharah svaritah (1.2.31), ‘svarita is bound together’, i.e. (i) and (ii)- : ‘ to pit© It is necessary to warn the user that these definitions do not necessarily refer 10 Pl 70 ८9 \# a ents, + Controversy surrounds this issue, as indeed it does the sounding of Greek ace 0 4 Pe . bject different traditions have their own interpretations, which themselves are sub} fluctuation. Hi ° man? ५ Owever, Pdnini’s Siksha (Sloka 48) comments on these accents निकः ers which suggests that the governing factor would seem to be the point at which t 4 . ‘ ऊ n vol attention or listening rests, a mental process forming a key part of the huma mechanism:- (i) anudatto hrdi jneyo, ‘know anudatta in the heart’. (ii) mirdnyudatta udahrtah, ‘udatta is declared to be in the cerebrum’. (iii) svaritah karnamaliyah, ‘svarita is the root of the ear’. + i\@ eaid to occu! (iv) sarvasye pracayahsmrtah, ‘pracaya svara (i.e. ekaSruti, ‘monotone’ is Sal in all places of articulation’. its Each section of the Dhdtu-Patha states the accent of the dhdtu monosyllable ee is anubandha suffix; these are the only two indications of accent given, i.e. no accen मी given for any dhdtu prefix, nor for dhdtvartha. It should be noted that the acce indicated for the anubandha suffixes is as follows: atmane — anudatta; parasmai — udatta; and ubhayatah ~ svarita. The various Dhatu-Patha 5 u, This edition of the Dhdtu-Patha text is generally attributed to Panini’s ordering of न i in the first millennium B.C.; the consensus of scholarly opinion seems to be tha ‘a tvartha, taken from traditional sources, were added near the middle of 4 an millennium A.D., probably by Bhimasena. Although these datings are extraordinar! y aa by European criteria, it must be remembered that the Indian tradition adopts a न ee Stance as regards precise dating. And there is good reason for this attitude, as wi - fe doubt a form of the Dhdtu-Patha, as with most of the other great texts of