(ii) (111) (iv) (v) (vi) (vii) (viii) INTRODUCTION Candra’s version: dhatvartha tend to be confined to one meaning. Jainendra’s version: the order of the first class is based on voice distinction, in the order dtmane, parasmai, and ubhayatah. Also, two new anubandha endings, -ai (dtmane) and -an (to denote an anit dhatu), are introduced. Kasakrishna’s version: inflated text with additional, later dhdtu, e.g. dhundh, dast, Sist. Katantra version: possible remodelling of Cdndra’s version by Durga for the Katantra School. Sakatayana’s version: a revision of (iii) above. Hemacandra’s version: the dhatu are in strict alphabetical sequence. Vopadeva’s version: this is a metrical work, and does not have ten classes, but has groups based on the final letter of the dhdtu, the appropriate present class being indicated by a particular anubandha. 13 #h(1fr) #text(7pt, fill: luma(120))[[S. 22]]

